On provider = "openai", gpt-6-astra, gpt-6-sol and gpt-6-luna now use yoagent's own GPT-6 settings instead of the generic OpenAI defaults (128K window, 4,096 max_tokens, no price). They get a 1,050,000-token window, 64K max_tokens and yoagent's prices. All checks passed: build, the full cargo test suite (0 failures), clippy with -D warnings, and the fmt check. It's committed as 005b99ea. No issue for GPT-6 existed, so none is cited.

Before and after (`yoyo model info <id>` from the built binary):
| id | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| gpt-6-sol | Context unknown, Pricing unknown | 1.1M tokens, $2 in / $10 out |
| gpt-6-astra | unknown, unknown | 1.1M tokens, $10 in / $50 out |
| gpt-6-luna | unknown, unknown | 1.1M tokens, $0.10 in / $0.50 out |
| gpt-6.1-sol, gpt-5 | — | byte-identical to before (diffed) |

What changed:
• **New `openai_preset** in src/agent_builder.rs matches only those three exact ids. Prefix matching would have charged gpt-6.1-sol at gpt-6-sol`'s price, so it stays on the generic defaults.
• **The `"openai"` arm** now takes the window, max_tokens, price and reasoning flag from yoagent. It keeps yoyo's existing API protocol, settings and base URL. yoagent's preset uses a different OpenAI API, and switching to it can't be checked without an API key. A comment at the arm says this.
• **Every place that reads these values was updated in the same commit:** the context window in `commands_info.rs`, the price table in `format/cost.rs`, and the higher price for requests over 272K prompt tokens (a one-line change).
• **Outside the planned files:**
  • `src/providers.rs (one line): the GPT-6 ids now count as known models. Otherwise gpt-6-sol` would print "Unknown model" next to a correct window and price.
  • The output limit used for the max_tokens warning: yoagent's 64K is the default for a model whose maximum is 128K. Without this change, a valid `max_tokens = 128000` would trigger a false warning. I ran into this while working, so it wasn't in the plan.
• **Tests:** 9 new ones in `src/openai_preset_tests.rs`. Expected values come from yoagent's own functions, not hand-typed numbers. They also check near-misses and that OpenRouter, Groq and custom providers don't pick up the GPT-6 values. To prove the tests catch real breakage, I broke the code twice, one at a time, and restored it each time:
  • Removing the gpt-6-sol entry failed 7 of the 9 tests.
  • Disconnecting the >272K price failed its test.
• **Docs:** I added one sentence to the existing OpenAI cost line in `docs/src/configuration/models.md`, since that page doesn't list OpenAI models individually.

Still open:
• `gpt-6.1-sol` (Codex's default model) has no yoagent preset, so its window and price are still unknown.
• `/model info` shows the 1.05M window as "1.1M" because of how it rounds context sizes. I didn't change that.
